Z||000000||Clint||12-13-1999||09:05 PM||||List,  A couple of weeks ago i managed to get a free 84 fiero.  I won't go into the details of how or where (worst trip ever for me as i wrecked the boss's new trailer i borrowed)  but none the less i now have the car and all it's problems. ;-)<P>The car will turn over and fire if you dump gas in it... it is not pumping gas and as i found out this weekend it has an electrical short somewhere.  I put a know good battery it in and charged it all day saturday since i was planning on working on it sunday afternoon.. But when i got to it sunday... not even a click. (the gauges moved a little.)<P>I'm pretty confident i can track down the fuel problem but i don't even know where to begin on the electrical short.<P>thought anyone?<BR>It's an 84 Auto.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Father-of-Fiero-owner||12-13-1999||09:27 PM||||maybe by chance the fusible link coming off the starter solenoid is burnt or the wire is broke. It happened to my sons and it wouldn't turnover and nothing that worked off the switch would work either(heater,radio,etc.) The fuel pump is in the gas tank and you should be able to hear it when you turn on the switch. With the fusible link gone the fuel wouldn't run either if I remember right. Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||12-13-1999||10:34 PM||||With everything off, keys out of ignition, take a multimeter, and check for current at every fuse on the fuse panel.  Only pull one fuse at a time so you know where to put it back in. Some fuses will have a small drain, (i.e. clock on radio, dome light when door is open, etc). If there is a current draw you know which circuit is is and can better track down the problem.<P>Electronics junk:<BR>Checking for voltage will mean nothing to you since anything hot will be around 12v(battery).<BR>To check for current you have to break the circuit(pull the fuse)and place multimeter in place of fuse to complete the circuit again.  Then the circuit that shows a current draw on your meter should be the one killing your battery.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-13-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-14-1999||07:05 PM||||no fuel........ check the fuel pump, its fuse, and relay. A shorted relay or pump could be part of the problem.<P>A fuse link will usually burn open if it gets grounded. but even if it hasn't they are easy to find.||||reg||1
